CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF CHILDREN, WOMEN, AND VICTIMS

Article 2

( 1) Every child has the right:
a. to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from criminal acts, violence, exploitation, and neglect;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. to worship according to their religion under the guidance of parents and to practice the teachings of their religion;

d . to know their parents, to be raised, and cared for by their own parents;

e . to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
f. to obtain education and teaching in accordance with age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence and interests, and talents;
g. to express and have their opinion heard and to receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with the level of intelligence and age for self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, play, express themselves for self-development;
1. to receive proper care from parents/guardians or other parties responsible for their care;
j. to obtain protection from abuse in politics, involvement in armed disputes, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence, involvement in warfare, targets of persecution, torture or imposition of inhumane punishment and involvement of children in the worst forms of work;
k. to obtain protection from the dangers of napza, pornography, and violent shows or other things that have an impact on changes in child development;

I. to have contact with parents if separated; and
m. to obtain other rights in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
2. Every woman has the right:a. to live, maintain life, and improve living standards;
b. to live peacefully, safely, peacefully, happily, prosperously physically and mentally;
c. to enjoy a good and healthy living environment;

d . to live in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations;

e . to obtain work according to ability, conditions and fair and just wages;
f. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to receive facilities and special treatment in public services;
g. to play an active role in the field of politics and government in accordance with the potential and capabilities they possess;
h. to obtain information and legal services; and
i. to obtain other rights in accordance with human dignity and based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Every victim has the right to receive:a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;

d . continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. assistance in accordance with applicable provisions;

g. guarantee of rights related to the victim's status as a family member and community member;

h . to obtain restitution in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
i. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 3

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 2 paragraph (1), and paragraph (3) also receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;

d . the right to be free from various stigma; and e . the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER III

OBLIGATIONS OF CHILDREN

Article 6

Every child is obliged to: a . respect parents, guardians, and teachers;
b. love family, community, and care for friends;
c. love the homeland, nation, and state;

d . perform worship in accordance with the teachings of their religion; and  e. practice noble ethics and morals.

Article 8

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 are implemented:a. to formulate and implement policies, programs, and activities for the protection of children and women;
b. to ensure the establishment and implementation of cooperation between various parties in the Region, with and between various parties in districts/cities and all related elements in the implementation of child and women protection;
c. to provide support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of child and women protection;

d . to fulfill, protect, and respect children's rights and ensure children to use their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the child's age and intelligence; and e . to foster and supervise the implementation of child and women protection.

2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) are coordinated by the Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for women's empowerment and child protection affairs.
3. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph ( 1), the Regional Government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of children and women in 1 (one) RAD as a basis for Regional Apparatus Organizations in implementing child and women protection in the region.
4. The RAD as referred to in paragraph (3) is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 11

( 1) The obligations and responsibilities of parents as referred to in Article 7, include:

a. caring for, maintaining, educating, providing a sense of security and not committing mistreatment and violence against children;
b. developing children in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. reporting every birth of a child to the agency authorized to register births;

d. preventing marriage at child age; and
e. providing character education and instilling moral values in children.

2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph ( 1) may be transferred to the family, which shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. In the event that the parents and family of the child cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph ( 1), and paragraph (2), shall be transferred to a person or legal entity that meets the requirements appointed as guardian of the child concerned.

Article 20

Implementation of protection of children and women is carried out by:

a. planning and development of information systems for children and women;

b. prevention and protection;

c. protection of children;

d. special protection;

e. protection of women;

f. protection in the workplace;

g. handling and recovery of victims of violence;

h. rehabilitation, reintegration, and empowerment;

i. law enforcement services and legal assistance; and

j. facilitation of juvenile justice.

Article 22

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to make efforts to prevent and protect against acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment, and neglect.

(2) Implementation of prevention and protection as referred to in paragraph (1), may cooperate with educational institutions, religious institutions, mass media, professional organizations, business entities, and/or community institutions.

(3) Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) are implemented by:

a. forming networks;

b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of prevention and protection based on partnership patterns;

c. establishing a system for preventing violence and protecting children and women;

d. disseminating Laws and Regulations related to the protection of women victims of violence;

e. providing education for victims of violence and vulnerable groups;

f. organizing family support in the form of family parenting education, child parenting education, family mediation, and economic support;

g. increasing the capacity of protection services for women and children including institutional capacity development and increasing the professionalism and competence of service providers;

h. increasing the ability of individuals and/or vulnerable groups to recognize risks and dangers from situations or acts that may cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment and neglect; and

i. increasing the utilization of communication, information, and education media.

(4) Prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 32

Education as referred to in article 31 is directed at:

a. development of attitudes and abilities of the child's personality, talents, mental abilities, and physical abilities to achieve optimal potential;

b. development of respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms;

c. development of respect for parents, cultural identity, language, and their own values, national values where the child resides, from where the child originates, and diverse customs of the child's own area of residence;

d. preparation of children for a responsible life; and

e. development of respect and love for the environment.

Article 38

1. The Regional Government in organizing maintenance and care is obliged to strive for and assist children to be able to:
a. participate;

JDIH Kota Singkawang
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the child's age stage and development;
d. freely associate and assemble;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.
2. The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are developed and adjusted to the child's age, level of ability and environment so as not to hinder and disturb the child's development.

Article 41

The Regional Government, community and other state institutions are obliged and responsible to provide special protection to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
JDIH Kota Singkawang
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immune deficiency syndrome;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;

j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;

l. children with special needs;
m. child victims of abuse and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

Article 42

Special protection for children is carried out through efforts to:
a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological and social rehabilitation as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;

b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for children from underprivileged families; and
d. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.

Article 43

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 41 letter a consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. child victims of riots;

c. child victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.

Article 46

Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 41 letter b is implemented through:
a. treatment of children in a humane manner taking into account needs according to their age;
b. separation from adults;
c. provision of legal assistance and other assistance effectively;
d. implementation of recreational activities;
e. freedom from torture, punishment or other treatment that is cruel, inhumane and degrading to their dignity and degree;
f. avoidance of imposition of the death penalty and/or life imprisonment;
g. avoidance of arrest, detention or imprisonment except as a last resort and for the shortest possible time;
h. provision of justice before an objective, impartial juvenile court and in a closed hearing to the public;
i. avoidance of publication of their identity;
j. provision of parental or guardian accompaniment and a person trusted by the child;
k. provision of social advocacy;

l. provision of private life;
m. provision of accessibility especially for children with disabilities;
n. provision of education;
o. provision of health services; and
p. provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

JDIH Kota Singkawang

Article 54

Special protection for child victims of sexual crimes as referred to in Article 41 letter j shall be carried out through efforts:  a. education on reproductive health, religious values, and moral values;
b. social rehabilitation;
c. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery; and
d. provision of protection and assistance at every level of examination from investigation, prosecution, to examination in court hearings.

Article 55

Special protection for child victims of terrorism networks as referred to in Article 41 letter k shall be carried out through efforts:  a. education on education, ideology, and nationalism values;  b. counseling on the dangers of terrorism;

c. social rehabilitation; and d. social assistance.

Article 62

1. For the prevention of violence against women, the Regional Government shall carry out empowerment and awareness to families and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

JDIH Kota Singkawang (2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government shall undertake the following efforts:
a. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
b. opening employment opportunities for women;
c. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women from violence;
d. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement officials, government officials, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women; and f. opening complaint posts for the protection of women from violence.

Bagi.an Seventh Protection in the Workplace

Article 64

1. The provisions as referred to in Article 63 paragraph (1), may be exempted by taking into account the following requirements:  a. age for child workers in informal sector work above 15 (fifteen) years;

b. obtaining written consent from the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector work;
c. implementation must be set forth in a written employment agreement between the company/employer and the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector work;

d. not employed at night;

e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;

f. not employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;
g. providing opportunities to socialize with family and the surrounding environment;

h. providing opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
i. employed for light types of work; and
j. providing health protection for children and the opportunity for one day off per week.
2. Protection for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (1), aims to:
a. prevent all forms of exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence against children; and
b. protect children from activities that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health.
3. Protection efforts for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (2), include among others:
a. providing counseling to the community about children's rights;
b. providing assistance in the form of psychological, medical and legal services to child workers in informal sector work who experience exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence;
c. empowering families through the provision of skills training and expenditure reduction;

d. providing scholarships to child workers in informal sector work who have dropped out of school to continue formal education to a higher level; and
e. providing non-formal education and skills training for child workers in informal sector work who do not pursue formal education.

Article 66

(1) Handling targets include:
a. children outside the care of parents;
b. children in disaster emergency situations;
c. children in conflict with the law;

d. child victims of physical, mental and sexual violence;
e. children subjected to abuse and neglect;
f. child victims of economic and sexual exploitation;
g. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse or things that cause dependence on narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances;
h. child victims of pornography;
i. children who have deviant social behavior;

j. children who experience stigmatization due to their parents' behavior;
k. children and women who are victims of human trafficking;

l. children and women who are in situations where the nature and conditions of the workplace can endanger health, safety and morals; and
m. women victims of physical, mental and sexual violence.
(2) Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:
a. identification process and receipt of complaints/reports;
b. rescue actions;
c. placement of children and women in temporary shelters;
d. rehabilitation including:
1. health recovery services;
2. psychosocial recovery services; and
3. legal assistance;
e. social reintegration.

Article 70

(1) Health recovery services as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter d number 1 include:
a. emergency services;
b. services for providing visum et repertum or psychiatric visum in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. follow-up services in the form of outpatient and/or inpatient care in accordance with medical provisions;
d. deoxyribose nucleic acid verification services;
e. delivery process services for pregnancy as a result of sexual violence and/or rape;
f. provision of further referral services in accordance with the victim's circumstances and condition; and
g. provision of special rooms separated from general patients.
2. The Regional Apparatus Organization responsible for health affairs is obliged to provide health recovery services.

Article 71

{ 1) Psychosocial recovery services as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter d number 2 include: a . psychological and social assessment as the basis for preparing treatment to be provided;
b. counseling;
c. psychological therapy and social rehabilitation programs;

d . mental and spiritual guidance;

e . assistance; and
f. provision of conducive and representative places for the implementation of psychosocial recovery service activities.

JDIH Kota Singkawang (2) The OPD responsible for social affairs is obligated to provide psychosocial recovery services.

Article 73

1. Social reintegration in the form of post-rehabilitation service support as referred to in Article 66 paragraph (2) letter e includes:  a . family member tracing;
b. reuniting children and family members/substitute families and/ or the community;

c . facilitation of psychosocial assistance for families;
d. support for access to further education or health services; and  e. monitoring and evaluation.

2. The OPD responsible for social affairs is obligated to provide social reintegration.

Article 77

(1) The Regional Government plays a role in facilitating the juvenile criminal justice process based on the principle of restorative justice.

JDIH Kota Singkawang (2) Facilitation of the juvenile criminal justice process as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a . capacity building of human resources whose duties are related to juvenile criminal justice and the community;
b. increased coordination with law enforcement officials;
c. development of effective methods through information support, facilities and positive activities and taking immediate action on gaps that encourage children and/ or groups of children to commit legal violations;
d. provision and delivery of legal and psychosocial assistance services in realizing the restorative justice and diversion process for children in conflict with the law;
e. encouraging a paradigm shift in society towards children in conflict with the law;
f. developing diversion mechanisms for children in conflict with the law in accordance with Laws and Regulations;
g. developing cooperation with relevant agencies or institutions in organizing education, coaching and guidance programs for handling children who commit criminal acts, especially children under 12 (two) years of age to achieve optimal development; andh. provision of facilities and infrastructure supporting the juvenile justice process by prioritizing the best interests of the child in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Further provisions regarding the facilitation of juvenile criminal justice as referred to in paragraph (1), and paragraph (2) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 78

( 1) Empowerment is implemented in the form of: a . facilitating the development of interests and talents for children and women;

JDIH Kota Singkawang
b. developing economic enterprises for adult women;
c. developing creative spaces for children and women; and  d. assistance for children and women victims of violence.
2. The implementation of empowerment as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with educational institutions, private institutions and/or non-governmental organizations.

CHAPTER X

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 79

( 1) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of organizing women's empowerment and child protection carried out comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of report/complaint handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.

2. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women, children and the quality of families;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; and
c. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
3. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is carried out through:
a. providing access for women and children to education, health and other strategic services;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural and other strategic fields;
e. establishment, development and capacity strengthening of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of human resources of managers;
g. strengthening institutional capacity for gender and child mainstreaming in the region;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
I. carrying out promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with prevailing laws and regulations.
4. In order to achieve comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government shall establish:
a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. a regional child protection commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; and
c. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of prevailing laws and regulations.

Article 83

1. The Regional Child Protection Commission has the following duties:
a. supervising the implementation of child protection and fulfillment of children's rights;
b. providing input and proposals in the formulation of policies regarding the implementation of child protection;
c. collecting data and information regarding child protection;
d. receiving and reviewing public complaints regarding violations of children's rights;
e. conducting mediation on disputes regarding violations of children's rights;
f. cooperating with institutions established by the community in the field of child protection;
g. providing reports to the authorities regarding alleged violations of the Child Protection Law; and
h. other duties in accordance with the provisions of prevailing laws and regulations.
2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Child Protection Commission shall be regulated by Mayor's Regulation.

Article 86

The development of KLA as referred to in Article 85, is implemented based on the following principles:
a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information disclosure, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not distinguishing ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;

c. best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child as the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;

d. the right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; and
e. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion, is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters affecting them.

Article 87

The development of KLA is directed at the fulfillment of children's rights which include:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;

d. education, leisure time, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.

Article 88

( 1) In order to realize KLA, the stages of KLA development are prepared which include:
a. preparation;
b. planning;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;

e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.
2. In each stage of KLA development, the views of children obtained through child consultation must be considered.

Article 90

(1) The main duties of the KLA Task Force are as follows:  a. coordinating various efforts for KLA development;
b. preparing the RAD for KLA development;
c. implementing socialization, advocacy and communication for KLA development;
d. monitoring the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the RAD for KLA development;
e. conducting evaluations at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the RAD for KLA development; and  f. submitting reports to the Mayor.
2. Further provisions regarding the KLA Task Force shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER XIV

GUIDANCE, SUPERVISION AND REPORTING

Article 94

(1) The Mayor has the authority to conduct guidance in the implementation of child and women protection.

2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in the form of: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;

c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and e. evaluation.

JDIH Kota Singkawang (3) In the context of guidance as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government may cooperate with the Central Government, other Regional Governments, Non-Governmental Organizations, business entities and universities.

4. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented through OPD according to their duties and functions.

CHAPTER XVI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 98

Every person is prohibited from:
a. receiving child visitors and employing children in discotheque businesses, nightclubs, bars, karaoke, pubs/music houses, massage parlors and steam baths/saunas, and/or other worst forms of child labor.  b. producing, selling and/or promoting cigarettes in the school environment.  c. selling and/or promoting cigarettes, gambling equipment, contraceptives and goods that can damage children's morals.  d. broadcasting and showing pornographic acts in community activities that can be accessed by children.  e. receiving child visitors during school hours or allowing child visitors after nine o'clock at night to access the internet at internet cafes.  f. refusing complaints and/or charging fees in providing services to victims at Protection Service Institutions.  g. treating children in a discriminatory manner that results in children experiencing losses both material and moral thereby hindering their social function;

h. treating children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
i. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of abuse and neglect;

J. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against children;
